</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:120%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE V: </span>Ablation analysis and component analysis on KIT-ML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> dataset. “R” denotes the retrieval strategy. “Banzhaf” and “Base” represents with and without Banzhaf interaction, respectively. “S” is the motion clips number. “N” indicates the prompt motions number. “M” indicates the utilization of motion clips or entire motion in motion prompt module.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ V-B1 Evaluation on Retrieval ‣ V-B Comparison with State-of-the-arts ‣ V Experiments ‣ MOST: Motion Diffusion Model for Rare Text via Temporal Clip Banzhaf Interaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> compares our approach with state-of-the-art T2M generation models. In comparison to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, we outperform current methods across all metrics for prompts with rareness in Tail 0-5%, 0-15%, and 0-25%. This highlights the generalizability of our proposed prompt-generation framework, showcasing its robustness in generating motion even under conditions of rare text. In contrast to the text-text retrieval-based method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, we demonstrate significant improvement for the Tail 0-5% prompts, indicating that integrating motion clips and motion prompt module information provides more valuable motion prompts to enhance the generation process. Overall, our MOST maintains high-quality generation even with rare and challenging texts, proving its remarkable generalization and robustness. Meanwhile, we have also conducted experiments on the HumanML3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and KIT-ML’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> common text prompts (Tail 75-100% prompts), as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ V-B1 Evaluation on Retrieval ‣ V-B Comparison with State-of-the-arts ‣ V Experiments ‣ MOST: Motion Diffusion Model for Rare Text via Temporal Clip Banzhaf Interaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, which demonstrates that MOST still has competitive performance under common text prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>4 </span>Evaluation on Assessing Motion Redundancy</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the extent to which the issue of motion redundancy has been addressed, we conducted related experiments with ReMoDiffuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> to verify our effectiveness, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ V-B2 Evaluation on Generation ‣ V-B Comparison with State-of-the-arts ‣ V Experiments ‣ MOST: Motion Diffusion Model for Rare Text via Temporal Clip Banzhaf Interaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. For a text <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we calculate the average Cosine similarity of the motion clips retrieved by MOST and ground truth motion data in the test sets, which are also calculated with the overall motion retrieved by ReMoDiffuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. We extracted the motion features using Guo’s pre-trained motion feature extractor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and calculated the average cosine similarity by:</p>
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ave}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{z}Cos&lt;F_{gt\_motion}^{i},F_{motion\_prompt}^{i}&gt;}{z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>z</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ave}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{z}Cos&lt;F_{gt\_motion}^{i},F_{motion\_prompt}^{i}&gt;}{z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ave}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ave}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes average cosine similarity, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> represents the number of samples in the dataset, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{gt\_motion}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{gt\_motion}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{motion\_prompt}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{motion\_prompt}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are ground truth motion data and retrieved motion prompts corresponding to text <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The results show that, compared to ReMoDiffuse’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> motion prompts of 196 frames, MOST only used the motion clips of 39 frames, which is only 20% of the length, significantly alleviating the problem of motion redundancy. At the same time, it achieved a higher average cosine similarity than ReMoDiffuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> on both the HumanML3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and KIT-ML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> datasets, demonstrating that MOST accurately captures the core content of the motions through carefully selected motion clips. This indicates that MOST not only solves the problem of motion redundancy but also obtains higher-quality motion prompts to optimize and improve the motion generation process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06590v1/minor/minor-userstudy.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/194;" width="476" height="194" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>The result of user study. Each bar represents the voting scores of methods, with higher values being better. The voting score ranges from 1 to 4.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06590v1/minor-compare_new.png" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/179;" width="476" height="179" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Visual results compared with existing methods.
The first line is the generated result on the rare text “blind”. The second line is the generated result of complex action combinations.
</figcaption>
</figure>
